 \begin{abstract}
 \noindent Climate-informed dengue early-warning systems are usually judged by discrimination (the area under the receiver-operating characteristic curve) and against less demanding comparators such as seasonal climatology. Public-health deployment, however, depends on whether predicted risks improve calibrated, threshold-based alert decisions beyond the information already available from routine surveillance.
 
-\noindent We conducted a retrospective decision-evaluation for short-horizon elevated-activity alerting in Sri Lanka (primary, RDHS-week, 2018--2025) and Colombia (a second, differently-constructed case study using a selected common-complete municipality subset, 2020--2022), comparing a recent-case surveillance baseline (M1) with climate-only and hybrid models by calibration, recalibration, and decision-curve net benefit ($h=4$, reference threshold $p^*=0.30$). Recent surveillance was a demanding benchmark: climate-only models did not outperform recent surveillance on the evaluated decision metrics, and the design-locked primary hybrid contrast in Sri Lanka did not establish a benefit and was calibration-sensitive: on raw predictions $\Delta$NB(M4$-$M1) $=-0.008$ (95\% CI $-0.028$ to $+0.013$), but because the hybrids (unlike M0--M3) were not recalibrated, an out-of-fold recalibration moved it to $+0.009$ (95\% CI including zero), so neither raw nor recalibrated contrasts established a hybrid benefit. In Colombia the full hybrid exceeded M1 by $+0.0188$, but M5 adds seasonal terms and department fixed effects M1 lacks; matching that structure reduced the climate increment to $+0.0078$ (95\% CI $+0.0039$ to $+0.0119$), about half the compound difference. This small increment did not grow with lead time and attenuated toward zero under a reporting-delay sensitivity (three-week matched $+0.0049$); its robustness to period could not be established, a development-inclusive refit bootstrap widened its interval to include zero. A specification-matched decomposition in Sri Lanka (frozen pipeline, validated) showed a similar small climate increment ($+0.0087$, interval including zero on raw predictions; $+0.015$ excluding zero under past-only recalibration), so the increment is small and of uncertain magnitude in both settings rather than confined to one. The two settings used differently constructed exposures (humidity in Sri Lanka only) and were not formally compared.
